// searchSnapshotCacheVersion names the on-disk cache directory and is hashed
// into every entry key, so bumping it moves new entries to a fresh directory
// and any prior-version directory can simply be deleted wholesale — cleanup is
// "remove old version dirs" instead of per-entry reachability analysis.
// v12 retires every entry produced before the final immutable-policy,
// unconditional-worktree-bypass transaction checks, and shared nested-ignore
// resource policy were complete; v13 retires entries whose DATA_FLOWS records
// carry a single evidence entry per edge rather than every flow; v14 retires
// entries written before truncated records counted what they dropped; v15
// retires the flat selective entries written before a derived snapshot was
// nested beneath the complete entry it came from, which would otherwise sit
// unreachable inside a live version directory and defeat that cleanup rule.
// v16 retires the diagnostic-count status in favor of source-file health.
// v17 retired entries written before symbols carried the parser's name line, which renderers
// anchor on; an older entry would silently send every symbol back to the text heuristic. v18
// retires v17 entries, whose name lines came from a producer that could record a wrong POSITIVE
// line (a return-type tag, a typedef alias's neighbour, a qualifier spelled like its member):
// nothing else in the key binds the producer's code — the provider version is "dev" for every
// local build — so only the namespace can keep those lines from being served as authoritative.
// Bump it again whenever the name-line producer's rules change.
// v19 retires route edges from the old sixteen-round parameter-taint cutoff.
// v20 retires warning text that incorrectly described every command family as worktree-default.
// v21 retires malformed-route literal guesses and undisclosed signature-budget loss.
// v22 retires checkout-path keys and keys the checkout-relative scope, so shared
// entries cannot cross repository subdirectories or replay older route producers.
// v23 retires routes omitted by closure-local writes and address escapes.
// v24 retires bare routes from wrapped mounts and unbounded route masks.
// v25 retires entries before unresolved route placement and Go 1.22 method patterns.
// v26 retires entries carrying DATA_FLOWS edges matched by another language's
// expression patterns; language-routed flow extraction no longer produces them.
// v27 retires entries carrying kind=tool symbols fabricated from body text (a
// duplicate definition of the handler's name); tool boundaries now come from
// identifiers only.
// v28 follows v27 and retires entries written before a failed
// co-change history read was disclosed. The query path never serves such an entry
// (its history refresh fails, which is a miss), but `graph store` and `graph
// context` read the persisted entry and its sqlite sidecar without building, so a
// pre-disclosure entry would hand them a header without
// W_COCHANGE_HISTORY_UNAVAILABLE. The sidecar sits beside the entry under this
// version's directory, so the bump orphans stale stores as well.
// v29 retires body-laden Julia signatures and module DATA_FLOWS edges.
const searchSnapshotCacheVersion = "search-snapshot-v29-" + IdentityRevision

// the sidecar's digest. Entries written by earlier builds embed the records
// inline and are unreadable at this version (and vice versa).
// v21 retires streams carrying DATA_FLOWS edges matched by another language's
// expression patterns (a Python conditional pattern on a Go body and the
// like); language-routed flow extraction no longer produces them.
// v22 retires streams carrying kind=tool symbols (and their HANDLES_TOOL edges)
// fabricated from body text; tool boundaries now come from identifiers only.
// v23 follows v22 and retires entries written before a failed
// co-change history read was disclosed. Such an entry is keyed exactly as this
// build keys the same failure (the history-unavailable scope and no pairs; the
// reason is not, and need not be, in the key), so without the bump this build
// replays its summary, which lacks W_COCHANGE_HISTORY_UNAVAILABLE: the silent
// drop the warning exists to end.
// v24 retires body-laden Julia signatures and module DATA_FLOWS edges.
const providerRecordsCacheVersion = "provider-records-v24-" + IdentityRevision

// cachedProviderRecords is the on-disk envelope for a cached record stream. The
// key alone is authoritative (sha256 over version+commit+tree+mode+profile+
// options+path-file contents), but we re-validate the discriminating fields on
// read as defense-in-depth against a stale or hand-edited cache file.
type cachedProviderRecords struct {
//
// Readers are never locked out. Graph's own readers and external consumers
// (docs/store-contract.md) open a store by path, read-only and immutable, so
// on POSIX an eviction unlinks the name while every open connection keeps
// reading the same inode, and the next resolution reports the store missing,
// which consumers already handle. Windows refuses to delete a file another
// process holds open; that removal error skips the store, which a later
// publish retries. Writers and evictors are serialized by
// searchStoreRetentionLockName, so an eviction can never remove a store that
// was republished under the same name after it was listed.
const (
	// searchStoreKeepEnv overrides searchStoreKeep. 0 or a negative value
	// turns retention off, which is the behaviour before it existed.
	searchStoreKeepEnv = "ENTIRE_GRAPH_SEARCH_STORE_KEEP"
	// defaultSearchStoreKeep is three: the store for HEAD plus two before it.
	// That covers a checkout that flips between two trees (a branch switch, a
	// rebase and back) and two or three worktrees of one repository indexing at
	// once, and leaves the nearest ancestors for a reader that falls back to
	// one. At the measured sizes it holds 204-588 MB per repository. Unbounded,
	// one store per first-parent commit came to about 1.2 GB a week on
	// entire-graph (17 commits) and 17 GB a week on entire-brain (86).
	defaultSearchStoreKeep = 3
	// searchStoreRetainBytes caps the recency window by size as well as by
	// count. At five times the largest store measured (196 MB) it never binds
	// before the count does on repositories of that size; it starts to bind
	// only where one store passes about 340 MB, at which point keeping older
	// copies costs more disk than rebuilding one from its gz entry costs time.
